course: dictionary
lesson: 13799
title: Investor
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: An investor is a person or company that puts money into a business, shares or property. They do this hoping to make a profit, which means getting back more money than they put in.
explain: Read the clues around the word. «Maya had a great idea for a bike lock, but she had no money to make it.» Then: «An investor gave her the money she needed.» The investor is the one who gives the money, not the one who asks for it.
explain: Check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence. «Ravi became an investor when he put money into his friend's bakery.» If you read it as "a person who puts money in", it still makes sense, so you have understood the word.
fact: An investor puts money into something, such as a business, shares or property.
fact: An investor usually hopes to get back more money than they put in. This extra money is called a profit.
fact: An investor can be a person or a company.
fact: On TV shows like «Dragons' Den» and «Shark Tank», inventors ask investors for money.
fact: Investors take a risk: the business may fail, and then they can lose their money.
exercise: «An investor gave Maya the money she needed for her bike lock.» What is an investor? | *A person or company that puts money into a business, hoping to make a profit | A teacher who helps with money sums | A shop that sells bikes | A person who makes bike locks
exercise: «The investor hoped the bike lock would sell well and make a profit.» What did the investor hope to get? | A pile of old bikes | A free lock for their home | A letter from Maya | *More money back than they put in
exercise: Find and copy the one word from this sentence that means a person or company that puts money into a business: «Ravi, an investor, bought a share in his friend's bakery.» | friend | *investor | share | bakery
test: Mia says, «My uncle is an investor in a company that makes shoes.» What does this tell you about her uncle? | He sells shoes in a shop on the high street | He makes shoes with his own hands | *He has put money into a company that makes shoes | He wears the company's shoes every day
test: Why might an investor study a business before giving it money? | *To check it could make a profit and pay back the money | Because the law says they must eat there first | To find out the colour of its logo | To get a free lunch from the owners
exam: Which sentence uses investor correctly? | A baker who borrows money from the bank is an investor | An investor is a person who bakes bread every morning | *A person who puts money into a bakery, hoping to share its profit, is an investor | Investors always live inside the bakery
